---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import WeekNumberCalculator from '@/components/tools/datetime/WeekNumberCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="week-number-calculator">
  <WeekNumberCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What the week number calculator does</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Date to week</strong> gives the ISO 8601 week number for any date, with the week's Monday and Sunday.</li>
      <li><strong>Week to dates</strong> gives all seven dates for a week number and year.</li>
      <li><strong>Whole year</strong> shows every week of a year in one scrollable table, with the current week marked.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The ISO 8601 rule</h2>
    <p>
      ISO 8601 defines week 1 of a year as the week that contains the first Thursday of January. The same rule
      in three equivalent forms:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Week 1 is the week containing 4 January.</li>
      <li>Week 1 is the first week with at least four days in the new year.</li>
      <li>Week 1 is the week containing the first Thursday of January.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Weeks always run Monday to Sunday, and a week belongs entirely to the year that contains its Thursday. So 30
      December can be in week 1 of the next year, and 1 January can be in week 52 or 53 of the previous one. The
      tool calls this the <strong>ISO week-numbering year</strong> and shows it when it differs from the calendar
      year.
    </p>

    <h2>52 or 53 weeks?</h2>
    <p>
      A 365-day year is 52 weeks plus one day; a leap year is 52 weeks plus two. The extra days add up, and every
      five or six years there is a 53rd week. Exactly: a year has 53 ISO weeks when 1 January is a Thursday, or
      when it is a Wednesday in a leap year.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Year</th>
            <th>1 January falls on</th>
            <th>ISO weeks</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>2024</td><td>Monday (leap year)</td><td>52</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2025</td><td>Wednesday</td><td>52</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2026</td><td>Thursday</td><td>53</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2027</td><td>Friday</td><td>52</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2028</td><td>Saturday (leap year)</td><td>52</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2029</td><td>Monday</td><td>52</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2030</td><td>Tuesday</td><td>52</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2031</td><td>Wednesday</td><td>52</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2032</td><td>Thursday (leap year)</td><td>53</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The 53-week year causes problems in retail and payroll: annual comparisons break, and weekly-paid staff get
      an extra pay run. Systems that assume 52 weeks often fail in those years.
    </p>

    <h2>ISO weeks versus US weeks</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>ISO 8601</th>
            <th>Common US / broadcast</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Week starts</td><td>Monday</td><td>Sunday</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Week 1 is</td><td>The week containing 4 January</td><td>The week containing 1 January</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Partial first week</td><td>Never: it belongs to the previous year</td><td>Yes: it can be one day long</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Weeks per year</td><td>52 or 53</td><td>52 or 53</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Used by</td><td>Europe, ISO, most software libraries</td><td>US business, broadcast and retail calendars</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The tool shows both numbers side by side. If a colleague's "week 33" does not match yours, this is almost
      always the reason. Check which convention their system uses before you reschedule anything.
    </p>

    <h2>Where week numbers are used</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Manufacturing and logistics: European supply chains schedule almost everything by calendar week ("KW" in
        German, "sem." in French).
      </li>
      <li>Project planning: sprint and milestone tracking in Gantt charts and roadmaps.</li>
      <li>Retail and finance: 4-4-5 and 52/53-week fiscal calendars use week numbers.</li>
      <li>Payroll: weekly and fortnightly pay cycles are usually numbered.</li>
      <li>Date stamping: many components are marked with a two-digit year and week of manufacture.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Writing week dates</h2>
    <p>
      The ISO format for a week is 2026-W33, and for a specific day 2026-W33-1, where the final digit is the day
      of the week, from Monday as 1 to Sunday as 7. This form is unambiguous and sorts correctly, so use it for
      filenames, report titles and anything a machine reads.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
